Looking at the code structure, I can identify the issue.

**Hypothesis: Event propagation is blocked unconditionally in Vortex handler**

In the Vortex component's `onPointerDown` at [elements/lib/react/scene/index.tsx](elements/lib/react/scene/index.tsx#L2590-L2610), the handler **calls `e.stopPropagation()` before checking the button**, unlike the Object handler:

```typescript
// Vortex (line 2590-2610) — STOPS ALL BUTTONS
const onPointerDown = useCallback(
    (e: { stopPropagation: () => void; nativeEvent: PointerEvent }) => {
        e.stopPropagation();  // ← Called unconditionally
        const pe = e.nativeEvent;
        if (pe.button !== 0) return;  // ← Button check happens AFTER
        ...
    }
);

// Object (line 2378-2390) — FILTERS FIRST
const handlePointerDown = useCallback(
    (e: { stopPropagation: () => void; nativeEvent?: PointerEvent }) => {
        const pe = e.nativeEvent;
        if (pe && pe.button !== 0) return;  // ← Button check FIRST
        e.stopPropagation();  // ← Only called for left-click
        ...
    }
);
```

When you right-drag, Vortex stops propagation before even checking the button, preventing the event from bubbling to OrbitControls for pan handling.

**Minimal fix to test**: Move `e.stopPropagation()` in [Vortex.onPointerDown](elements/lib/react/scene/index.tsx#L2593) to **after** the `if (pe.button !== 0) return;` check at [line 2597](elements/lib/react/scene/index.tsx#L2597), matching the Object handler pattern. This allows right-drag events to propagate to OrbitControls while still capturing left-clicks properly.

